WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.305
Lesson 3.6.

00:00:02.305 --> 00:00:05.090
Lending design deep dive: e-mode, caps,

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.245
auctions, soft liquidation, and bad debt.

00:00:08.245 --> 00:00:10.326
By the end, you'll read a lending

00:00:10.326 --> 00:00:12.896
protocol's risk parameters the way a risk

00:00:12.896 --> 00:00:15.160
manager does, not just as a borrower.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:17.750
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:21.472
Efficiency mode, and isolation mode, the two ways protocols

00:00:21.472 --> 00:00:25.060
let some collateral behave differently from the rest.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.801
Supply and borrow caps, the

00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:29.650
limits that protect against manipulation.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:31.156
The three different ways a

00:00:31.156 --> 00:00:33.465
protocol can actually liquidate you.

00:00:33.465 --> 00:00:35.981
And who pays when collateral ends up worth

00:00:35.981 --> 00:00:38.087
less than the debt it backs, plus a

00:00:38.087 --> 00:00:40.310
real worked example of e-mode failing.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.830
Efficiency mode, and isolation mode.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:49.305
Here's what efficiency mode, usually called e-mode, actually does.

00:00:49.305 --> 00:00:52.830
It's a special setting for collateral and debt that

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.355
are expected to move together, like a staked E.T.H.

00:00:56.355 --> 00:01:00.925
token against E.T.H. itself, or one stablecoin against another.

00:01:00.925 --> 00:01:03.858
Because the protocol expects those two prices to

00:01:03.858 --> 00:01:06.851
stay close, it allows a much higher loan-to-value

00:01:06.851 --> 00:01:09.246
than it would for unrelated assets.

00:01:09.246 --> 00:01:12.071
That's genuinely useful for correlated loops.

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:15.076
But it's dangerous specifically if that correlation

00:01:15.076 --> 00:01:17.329
breaks, meaning if the two assets ever

00:01:17.329 --> 00:01:19.756
trade apart from each other, even briefly.

00:01:21.056 --> 00:01:24.416
Isolation mode does something almost the opposite.

00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:27.106
Instead of raising what one asset can do, it

00:01:27.106 --> 00:01:29.976
contains what a risky one is allowed to do.

00:01:29.976 --> 00:01:33.713
A newer or less-proven collateral type gets isolated: it

00:01:33.713 --> 00:01:36.860
can only back a limited amount of borrowing, on

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.586
its own, separate from the main pool.

00:01:39.586 --> 00:01:42.748
That way, if that specific asset fails badly, the

00:01:42.748 --> 00:01:46.480
damage stays contained to its own isolated market, instead

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.832
of spreading into everything else the protocol holds.

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:52.223
Quick check.

00:01:52.223 --> 00:01:53.758
What's e-mode actually for?

00:01:58.178 --> 00:02:03.864
The answer: allowing higher loan-to-value ratios specifically for correlated

00:02:03.864 --> 00:02:08.073
assets, like a staked E.T.H. token against E.T.H. itself.

00:02:09.373 --> 00:02:12.157
Here's how to actually check whether e-mode fits

00:02:12.157 --> 00:02:14.388
a position, before you rely on it.

00:02:14.388 --> 00:02:17.250
Confirm the two assets are genuinely expected

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.728
to track, not just similarly named.

00:02:19.728 --> 00:02:22.326
Check what oracle prices each one, since that

00:02:22.326 --> 00:02:24.998
decides whether a brief panic reaches you.

00:02:24.998 --> 00:02:27.823
Ask what happens to your position specifically

00:02:27.823 --> 00:02:30.468
if that correlation gaps, even briefly.

00:02:30.468 --> 00:02:32.790
And only then decide whether the higher

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.228
loan-to-value is worth that specific risk.

00:02:36.528 --> 00:02:39.311
Worth being precise about what isolation mode

00:02:39.311 --> 00:02:42.215
actually contains, since it's a boundary around

00:02:42.215 --> 00:02:44.693
the asset, not a guarantee about it.

00:02:44.693 --> 00:02:47.229
An isolated market can still fail on

00:02:47.229 --> 00:02:49.559
its own terms, drop in value, get

00:02:49.559 --> 00:02:52.258
liquidated, even run into bad debt.

00:02:52.258 --> 00:02:55.077
What isolation mode prevents is that failure

00:02:55.077 --> 00:02:57.521
from being able to touch collateral in

00:02:57.521 --> 00:03:00.653
completely unrelated markets on the same protocol.

00:03:01.953 --> 00:03:03.543
Supply and borrow caps.

00:03:04.843 --> 00:03:07.705
Here's what caps actually protect against, and

00:03:07.705 --> 00:03:10.867
why they exist even on well-established assets.

00:03:10.867 --> 00:03:12.982
A supply cap limits how much of

00:03:12.982 --> 00:03:15.360
a given asset can be deposited into

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.907
the protocol as collateral, total.

00:03:17.907 --> 00:03:19.780
A borrow cap limits how much of

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.187
an asset can be borrowed out, total.

00:03:22.187 --> 00:03:26.262
Both exist specifically to protect against price manipulation on

00:03:26.262 --> 00:03:29.835
thin markets, and against a situation where the protocol

00:03:29.835 --> 00:03:33.095
holds more of an illiquid asset than could actually

00:03:33.095 --> 00:03:35.477
be sold if it needed to be liquidated.

00:03:36.776 --> 00:03:39.790
Worth being precise about who actually sets these

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.466
numbers, since it isn't automatic or purely algorithmic.

00:03:43.466 --> 00:03:47.711
Risk curators, or risk managers, set the specific loan-to-value

00:03:47.711 --> 00:03:51.956
ratios, liquidation thresholds, and caps for each market, based

00:03:51.956 --> 00:03:55.241
on that asset's own liquidity and volatility.

00:03:55.241 --> 00:03:59.381
Protocol governance then reviews and approves those parameters.

00:03:59.381 --> 00:04:02.225
Both roles matter: read the parameters, not

00:04:02.225 --> 00:04:04.746
just the protocol's name or reputation.

00:04:06.046 --> 00:04:07.671
Three ways to get liquidated.

00:04:08.971 --> 00:04:11.154
Here's the first two, side by side,

00:04:11.154 --> 00:04:14.547
since they represent genuinely opposite trade-offs.

00:04:14.547 --> 00:04:17.971
A fixed bonus liquidation pays the liquidator a set

00:04:17.971 --> 00:04:21.989
extra amount, commonly around five percent of the collateral

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:24.857
seized, as their incentive to act fast.

00:04:24.857 --> 00:04:27.957
It's simple and reliably fast, but that bonus is

00:04:27.957 --> 00:04:31.547
a fixed cost to you regardless of market conditions.

00:04:31.547 --> 00:04:35.021
A Dutch auction instead lets the price fall gradually

00:04:35.021 --> 00:04:38.689
until somebody actually buys, which can produce a better

00:04:38.689 --> 00:04:41.777
price in a calm market, but depends entirely on

00:04:41.777 --> 00:04:45.637
liquidator bots, called keepers, actually showing up to bid.

00:04:46.937 --> 00:04:50.180
And here's the third mechanism, soft liquidation, which

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.912
works differently from both of the others.

00:04:52.912 --> 00:04:55.341
Instead of seizing your collateral all at

00:04:55.341 --> 00:04:57.655
once with a single penalty, it converts

00:04:57.655 --> 00:05:00.316
your collateral gradually, across a series of

00:05:00.316 --> 00:05:02.872
price bands as the price keeps falling.

00:05:02.872 --> 00:05:05.927
And critically, that conversion can actually reverse:

00:05:05.927 --> 00:05:08.303
if the price recovers before you're fully

00:05:08.303 --> 00:05:11.432
liquidated, some of that collateral converts back.

00:05:11.432 --> 00:05:14.813
Your losses come from the individual conversions along

00:05:14.813 --> 00:05:17.517
the way, not from one single, fixed penalty.

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:19.907
Quick check.

00:05:19.907 --> 00:05:21.985
How does soft liquidation actually

00:05:21.985 --> 00:05:24.242
differ from a fixed-bonus liquidation?

00:05:28.662 --> 00:05:32.812
The answer: collateral is converted gradually across price bands,

00:05:32.812 --> 00:05:36.019
and can convert back if price recovers, instead of

00:05:36.019 --> 00:05:38.912
being seized all at once with a fixed penalty.

00:05:40.212 --> 00:05:41.937
Bad debt: who actually pays.

00:05:43.237 --> 00:05:46.182
Here's what happens when liquidation genuinely can't

00:05:46.182 --> 00:05:48.293
keep up, and collateral ends up worth

00:05:48.293 --> 00:05:50.482
less than the debt it was backing.

00:05:50.482 --> 00:05:52.962
That shortfall is called bad debt, and it

00:05:52.962 --> 00:05:56.037
doesn't just vanish; somebody has to absorb it.

00:05:56.037 --> 00:05:58.055
Some protocols hold a reserve or

00:05:58.055 --> 00:06:00.802
an insurance fund specifically for this.

00:06:00.802 --> 00:06:03.628
Others have a staking safety module, where staked

00:06:03.628 --> 00:06:06.132
tokens can be slashed to cover the gap.

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:09.639
And some protocols instead socialise the loss, spreading

00:06:09.639 --> 00:06:12.100
it pro rata across all of their lenders.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.829
Worth being precise about why this question matters before

00:06:16.829 --> 00:06:20.325
you lend anywhere, not just after something goes wrong.

00:06:20.325 --> 00:06:22.620
Knowing who absorbs bad debt in a given

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.547
protocol tells you what you're actually exposed to

00:06:25.547 --> 00:06:28.129
as a lender, even if you've never personally

00:06:28.129 --> 00:06:29.921
borrowed a dollar from it.

00:06:29.921 --> 00:06:33.031
A protocol with no reserve and no safety module

00:06:33.031 --> 00:06:36.271
is quietly asking its lenders to be that backstop.

00:06:37.571 --> 00:06:38.576
Worked example.

00:06:39.876 --> 00:06:42.231
Here's the scenario, step by step.

00:06:42.231 --> 00:06:45.508
An L.S.T., a liquid staking token, is looped

00:06:45.508 --> 00:06:50.031
against E.T.H. in e-mode, at ninety percent loan-to-value.

00:06:50.031 --> 00:06:53.086
It looks safe, because the L.S.T. and E.T.H.

00:06:53.086 --> 00:06:56.781
are expected to move together, almost in lockstep.

00:06:56.781 --> 00:07:00.046
Then, during a market panic, the L.S.T. trades

00:07:00.046 --> 00:07:03.241
at a six percent discount specifically on the

00:07:03.241 --> 00:07:06.736
oracle the protocol actually uses to price it.

00:07:06.736 --> 00:07:09.132
That discount alone is enough to push

00:07:09.132 --> 00:07:11.906
the position past its liquidation threshold.

00:07:13.206 --> 00:07:15.164
Here's the actual lesson this scenario

00:07:15.164 --> 00:07:17.271
proves, and it's worth sitting with.

00:07:17.271 --> 00:07:21.747
The position gets liquidated even though E.T.H.'s own price

00:07:21.747 --> 00:07:25.552
didn't move at all; only the LST's price, relative

00:07:25.552 --> 00:07:28.986
to E.T.H., on that specific oracle, moved.

00:07:28.986 --> 00:07:31.083
The correlation e-mode depends on

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:32.801
isn't a law of physics.

00:07:32.801 --> 00:07:35.218
It's a market relationship that can gap,

00:07:35.218 --> 00:07:37.399
even briefly, and the oracle is what

00:07:37.399 --> 00:07:40.111
actually decides whether that gap reaches you.

00:07:41.411 --> 00:07:44.932
So the one thing worth checking before trusting any

00:07:44.932 --> 00:07:48.996
e-mode position is exactly which oracle prices your L.S.T.,

00:07:48.996 --> 00:07:53.291
since the two options behave very differently under stress.

00:07:53.291 --> 00:07:56.865
A market-price oracle reflects whatever the L.S.T.

00:07:56.865 --> 00:07:59.318
is actually trading at on the open

00:07:59.318 --> 00:08:02.141
market right now, discount included.

00:08:02.141 --> 00:08:06.973
An exchange-rate oracle instead reflects the token's underlying redemption

00:08:06.973 --> 00:08:10.968
value, which doesn't move just because trading briefly panics.

00:08:12.268 --> 00:08:13.358
Quick check.

00:08:13.358 --> 00:08:15.233
Who can end up paying for bad debt?

00:08:19.653 --> 00:08:23.312
The answer: a protocol's reserve or insurance fund,

00:08:23.312 --> 00:08:26.618
a staking safety module, or the protocol's own

00:08:26.618 --> 00:08:29.503
lenders, on a pro rata, socialised basis.

00:08:30.803 --> 00:08:32.263
Here's your checklist.

00:08:32.263 --> 00:08:33.645
Do it for every market

00:08:33.645 --> 00:08:35.328
you use, not just once.

00:08:35.328 --> 00:08:39.015
For each market: know its loan-to-value, its liquidation

00:08:39.015 --> 00:08:42.766
threshold, its liquidation mechanism and bonus, its caps,

00:08:42.766 --> 00:08:45.653
and specifically which oracle prices it.

00:08:45.653 --> 00:08:47.838
Know who actually absorbs bad debt

00:08:47.838 --> 00:08:49.948
in each protocol you lend to.

00:08:49.948 --> 00:08:52.598
And give every e-mode position of yours its

00:08:52.598 --> 00:08:55.188
own depeg scenario in your own stress test.

00:08:56.488 --> 00:08:57.733
Let's recap.

00:08:57.733 --> 00:09:00.910
E-mode raises LTV for correlated assets, but

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:03.893
is exposed if that correlation breaks.

00:09:03.893 --> 00:09:06.454
Isolation mode contains a risky asset's

00:09:06.454 --> 00:09:08.418
failure to its own market.

00:09:08.418 --> 00:09:12.168
Caps protect against manipulation and illiquid collateral.

00:09:12.168 --> 00:09:16.219
Liquidation mechanisms differ: fixed bonus, Dutch auction,

00:09:16.219 --> 00:09:19.403
or soft liquidation with reversible bands.

00:09:19.403 --> 00:09:21.478
And bad debt has to go somewhere,

00:09:21.478 --> 00:09:23.859
a reserve, a safety module, or lenders

00:09:23.859 --> 00:09:26.483
themselves, so know which, before you lend.

00:09:27.783 --> 00:09:31.657
Check the parameters, not just the protocol's name: LTV,

00:09:31.657 --> 00:09:36.103
threshold, mechanism, caps, oracle, and who absorbs bad debt.

00:09:36.103 --> 00:09:38.347
Next up, Lesson 3.7: CDP

00:09:38.347 --> 00:09:41.758
stablecoins, minting your own dollars.
